WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Open Access, or OA, operates on a
straightforward principle.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.000
Knowledge created with public resources
should be freely available to the

3
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:11.900
public. Major funding agencies and
governments are actively pushing for this

4
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.320
model. This diagram illustrates the
dilemma early career scholars face.

5
00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.460
On one side, we have the societal benefit
of open science.

6
00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:25.100
On the other, the reality of career
advancement, which is heavily tied to

7
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.840
publishing in highly prestigious
traditional journals that locked their

8
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.660
content behind paywalls. If a younger
researcher chooses an open access venue,

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.540
they often end up in newer, less cited
journals.

10
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.860
In the competitive world of academic
hiring and promotion, that choice carries

11
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.420
substantial professional risk. By tying
job security to traditional journal

12
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.120
rankings, the publishing ecosystem forces
researchers into an ethical

13
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.320
compromise between serving the public good
and ensuring dirt on survival in

14
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:53.660
the field.
